WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.640
Forecast for more than 6,500 new earth observation satellites by 2035, highlight why rapid reusable launch capacity will determine who captures the coming manufacturing surge. This is SpaceX Daily, episode 78. This week brought updates on starbase expansion plans in South Texas. Confirmation that NASA's Dragonfly mission will ride a falcon heavy in 2028 and continued regulatory steps for starlink in South Africa.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:55.780
NovaSpace projects more than 6,500 earth observation satellites will reach orbit by 2035, generating 155.9 billion dollars in manufacturing revenue. Governments are driving much of the demand through expanded sovereign intelligence and surveillance programs. The forecast covers both government and commercial constellations that will require frequent, cost-effective rides to orbit.

00:00:55.780 --> 00:01:02.820
Reusable medium and heavy lift vehicles are positioned to handle the bulk of these deployments given the projected cadence.

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:22.860
Paris-based analysts released the numbers this week after reviewing government procurement pipelines and commercial operator filings through the early 2030s S. The 155.9 billion dollars manufacturing figure reflects average satellite costs across optical, radar, and hyperspectral payloads.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:36.920
California startup LV has completed spaceflight qualification of its 100 watt millimeter wave traveling wave tube amplifier platform. The work was supported by US Space for Small Business Innovation Research funding.

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:51.420
Qualification testing addressed the vacuum, thermal, and radiation environment of low earth orbit. The amplifiers target high bandwidth satellite communications links where millimeter wave frequencies enable higher data rates than traditional bands.

00:01:51.420 --> 00:02:12.160
Engineers at LV subjected multiple flight representative units to thermal vacuum cycling and total ionizing dose radiation exposure at levels expected in sun synchronous orbits. The 100 watt output specification was verified across the full temperature range while maintaining spectral purity required for dense frequency reuse.

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:24.780
Area in a space has dropped previously announced performance upgrades for the area and six launch vehicle. The decision leaves the rocket at its current payload capacity without the incremental improvements once planned.

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:38.920
European operators now face a narrower set of options for medium heavy payloads until a next generation vehicle arrives. The baseline two core configuration will therefore remain the sole operational variant for the foreseeable future.

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:50.520
Industry sources indicate the cancelled upgrades would have added roughly one ton to geo stationary transfer orbit performance. No public cost figure for an area and six mission has been released.

00:02:50.520 --> 00:03:07.040
Observers on our slash space X lounge are examining what the planned retirement of Falcon 9 means for Vandenberg's future launch pads noting that SLC 6 has already seen shuttle era structures removed, yet no starship infrastructure has been announced there.

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:17.600
Users point out that Vandenberg could surpass Cape Canaveral in total space X launches this year and worry the site may go quiet once Falcon operations wind down.

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:28.320
A separate post revisits the logistics of recovering ship 40 with contributors debating the value of returning the vehicle to Texas versus leaving it on a heavy lift vessel.

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:41.000
Worth keeping an eye on is Europe's decision to cancel area and six upgrades which removes near term competition on certain medium payload missions but also signals slower European launch cadence overall.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:59.700
Without the improvements area and space will rely on the baseline vehicle configuration for the foreseeable future. The move could shift some government payloads toward US providers, yet it simultaneously underscores that European sovereign launch capability remains constrained until a successor system is fielded.

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:06.180
Resolution depends on whether a follow on European rocket program secures funding and accelerates development.

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:25.080
On the AI front, no new source developments appear today on the SpaceX AI GROC cursor compute thread. The live threads to watch remain orbital data center concepts direct to sell backhaul, colossus scale cluster expansion and GROC distribution inside developer tools.

00:04:25.580 --> 00:04:34.080
In the engineering deep dive this week, millimeter wave traveling wave tube amplifiers solve a fundamental link budget problem in satellite communications.

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:48.760
At these frequencies, the available spectrum is far wider than at S or Kuban, so the same aperture can carry many times more bits per second, yet free space path loss rises sharply and atmospheric absorption becomes material.

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:59.500
A 100 watt TWT must therefore deliver that power while surviving the vacuum, thermal cycling and radiation environment without excessive mass or heat rejection.

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:11.400
Qualification testing therefore focuses on cathode life, helix thermal management and collector efficiency, because every watt not converted to RF output must be radiated away.

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:24.800
Once qualified, the amplifier enables tighter beams and higher order modulation on small satellites, directly lowering the cost per delivered bit and allowing denser constellations to share spectrum without mutual interference.

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:42.480
The same physics that makes millimeter wave attractive also explains why optical links are being pursued in parallel. Both approaches trade aperture size and pointing precision against raw bandwidth, but the TWT path avoids the cloud and daylight constraints that still limit laser terminals.

00:05:42.480 --> 00:06:02.520
In first principles terms, the raw material cost of the helix collector and cathode assembly is only a few hundred dollars, yet the finished space qualified unit commands orders of magnitude more because of the precision machining, vacuum brazing and multi-month test campaign required to guarantee 20 year reliability.

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:14.420
That gap is the classic idiot index for RF hardware. Each successful qualification run collapses the index by proving the design can be reproduced at lower cost on the next batch.

00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:24.140
Elves SBIR funded path therefore represents a deliberate attempt to move the index downward for the entire industry rather than for a single mission.

00:06:24.140 --> 00:06:32.180
The net result is a practical middle ground that lets operators increase throughput without waiting for fully optical networks to mature.

00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:46.020
For constellations already sized around thousands of satellites, even a 20% improvement in bits per watt at the amplifier stage translates into measurable reductions in required solar array area and battery mass.

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:55.400
Those mass savings compound across every launch, directly affecting how many additional earth observation payloads can ride on the same reusable booster.

00:06:55.980 --> 00:07:15.080
Over the 2026 to 2035 window projected by NOVA space, the cumulative effect of such incremental hardware gains will determine whether the 155.9 billion dollars manufacturing forecast can be realized within existing launch economics or whether new capacity constraints appear.

